  "question": {
    "text": "A function $y=f(x)$ has a second order derivative $f^{\\prime \\prime}(x)=6(x-1)$. If its graph passes through the point $(2,1)$ and at that point the tangent to the graph is $y=3 x-5$, then the function is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "zaoqs",
      "text": "\n$(x-1)^2$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "01chG",
      "text": "\n$(x-1)^3$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "heafx",
      "text": "\n$(x+1)^3$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "MSqOb",
      "text": "\n$(x+1)^2$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$f^{\\prime \\prime}(x)=6(x-1) \\Rightarrow f^{\\prime}(x)=3(x-1)^2+c$\nand $f^{\\prime}(2)=3 \\Rightarrow c=0$\n$\\Rightarrow f(x)=(x-1)^3+k$ and $f(2)=1 \\Rightarrow k=0$\n$\\Rightarrow f(x)=(x-1)^3$",
    "image": null
  },
